  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/boraras_naevus_en/",
      "html": "<p>The dwarf rasbora that has been traded for some years under the name \"Boraras micros RED\" has now been scientifically described and named Boraras naevus. The species is distinguished from all other spotted Boraras species known so far by sexual dichromatism. Boraras naevus reaches a maximum length of 1.5–2 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/boraras_naevus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Origin: Thailand.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/boraras_naevus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Regarding keeping and breeding, these charming dwarfs do not differ in any way from their congeners. They should be kept in small, well planted tanks with a dark substrate in soft and acidic water. Under these conditions the dwarf rasboras are astonishingly long-living and can be enjoyed for many years.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/boraras_naevus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males develop a much bigger shoulder spot than females.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/boraras_naevus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Boraras: anagram of Rasbora, referring to the reverse ratio of abdominal and caudal vertebrae in Boraras compared with Rasbora. naevus: Latin, means \"spot\" or \"mark on the skin\"; refers to the prominent shoulder spot of the species.</p>"
    }
  ],
